  "findings": [
    "Application mode discovers the MCP tool successfully without login. A real model calls the native write route once; the MCP server returns isError with mcp/www_authenticate metadata. No auth view appears; a second model request follows the failed result.",
    "Installing the public MCPManager.setAuthHandler from a diagnostic session_start extension does not fix the initial connection path. The test separately confirms the extension installed its handler.",
    "The same handler receives the exact scope challenge after an explicit native reconnect. The control intentionally returns undefined, so this verifies parameter delivery only, not successful OAuth.",
    "The refreshed control reconnects then refreshes the server tool list through public SDK methods. The handler again receives nothing.",
    "SDK manager.ts startup fulfilled/cached tool registration and refreshServerToolsOnce construct reconnect callbacks without forwarding options; doReconnect/new startup background path preserve authChallenge. No SDK files were edited.",
    "The production OmpMcpAuth currently supports only idle manual login. A complete fix also needs an installed native auth handler, waitForUser behavior, in-task presentation/cancellation, and preservation of the native single-tool retry instead of host prompt replay."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Diagnostic controls stop at challenge delivery; no real account or completed OAuth grant.",
    "Existing manual OAuth regression and full smoke do not cover this intentionally failing diagnostic.",
    "No forced reconnect workaround or SDK patch has been added to production.",
    "This is an OMP migration gap, not the Windows DeepSeek root-cause result."
  ],
  "safety": [
    "Temporary agent/workspace, isolated local HTTP model/MCP servers, fake credentials only.",
    "Final four runs deny non-loopback worker fetches and remove inherited API keys/tokens/proxy variables.",
    "No user service, SDK files, live histories or project files modified."
  ],
